From 9451f39bdf21b263a50172c0f7a58691f8573631 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:01:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(distribution): open the free tier to agents, and make
 the reference the thing a model cites
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0018. The choke point was not the product. It was that the best channel to
it was bolted shut.

THE MCP TRANSPORT WAS KEY-GATED END TO END

Every paid tool inside it already checked its own tier. So the gate protected
nothing — and closed the single best distribution channel this product has: a
stranger adding GridForge to their AI client and getting a real engineering answer
about their own hall in thirty seconds, with the binding constraint named.

The transport is public now; each tool keeps its own tier. gridforge_qualify needs
no key and no account. Every paid tool still refuses without one, and names the free
tool when it does, so an agent always knows what it CAN call. tools/list shows
everything whatever the caller holds: an agent that cannot see a paid tool cannot
tell its user the answer exists, which costs us more than it costs them.

The paid tiers sell because the free one already answered something true.

One paste, on /developers, for Claude Code, Claude Desktop, with or without a key,
and as raw curl:

  claude mcp add --transport http gridforge https://gridforge-engine.fly.dev/mcp

The handshake instructions are load-bearing and were rewritten to earn it: the free
tool and what it needs, an instruction not to present an E0 or E1 figure as a
property of a physical asset, and the fact that the calibration record currently
reads uncalibrated. A modelled number loose inside an agent loop is more dangerous
than one in a board pack — nobody downstream reads the footnote.

BEING THE SOURCE A MODEL CITES

/llms.txt — the machine index. What the site contains, where the canonical JSON
lives, how to read an evidence class, how to call the free tool, and the caveat to
carry with any figure quoted. Generated from the same catalogue the pages render,
so it cannot go stale.

/reference/constraints/<slug>.json — a machine-readable twin per constraint, each
carrying the notice. A crawler scraping rendered HTML gets the prose and loses the
evidence class, the lead time and the arithmetic. A twin travelling without its
notice would be a naked number, so CI checks for it.

/api/cite — the citation, the BibTeX, and the required caveat in one call. The
friction that stops a niche citing you is clerical: nobody knows what to put in the
footnote. So we write it, and the footnote carries the caveat.

/api/constraints — the same data behind a query parameter, for anything that would
rather call an endpoint than fetch a file.

robots.txt now welcomes AI crawlers EXPLICITLY — GPTBot, OAI-SearchBot, ClaudeBot,
PerplexityBot, Google-Extended, Applebot-Extended, CCBot and the rest — while every
token-addressed path stays closed to all of them. Most of this niche is blocking
them. We want the opposite: when an operator asks a model what stops their hall
taking AI racks, better that the answer comes from a constraint reference that names
its evidence class than from a vendor reference design that ends in a bill of
materials. That is not altruism and the runbook does not describe it as such.

TESTS

15 new (346 total). The free tool answers with no key and leaks nothing priced;
every paid tool refuses with the free one named; the handshake says what a model
must not do; llms.txt teaches the evidence ladder and is generated rather than
typed; every constraint has a twin carrying its notice; no crawler may reach a
token-addressed path.

One existing test asserted the opposite of the new contract — that /mcp required a
key — and is replaced rather than deleted, with the reasoning written down.

